𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟭/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗗𝗲𝗯𝘂𝗴𝗴𝗶𝗻𝗴 & 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on something every DevOps Engineer needs to become comfortable with — debugging Ansible playbooks.

Even well-written automation can fail. The important skill is knowing where and why it failed.

🔍 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗺𝗼𝗻 𝗗𝗲𝗯𝘂𝗴𝗴𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗧𝗼𝗼𝗹𝘀

𝗗𝗲𝗯𝘂𝗴 𝗠𝗼𝗱𝘂𝗹𝗲
Useful for printing variables and understanding what Ansible is seeing.

- name: Show variable
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    var: my_variable

𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗠𝗼𝗱𝗲

Before making changes:

ansible-playbook site.yml --check

𝗗𝗶𝗳𝗳 𝗠𝗼𝗱𝗲

See what will change:

ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff

𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗯𝗼𝘀𝗲 𝗢𝘂𝘁𝗽𝘂𝘁

For deeper troubleshooting:

ansible-playbook site.yml -vvv

Ansible verbosity levels can help identify problems with variables, connections, modules, and task execution.

🛠️ 𝗔 𝗦𝗶𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the failed task
2️⃣ Read the error message carefully
3️⃣ Verify variables
4️⃣ Check inventory and connectivity
5️⃣ Use debug when needed
6️⃣ Run with -vvv for detailed output
7️⃣ Fix → test → run again

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Don't just look at what failed.

Understand why it failed.

Good Ansible automation isn't only about writing playbooks — it's also about being able to troubleshoot them quickly in real-world environments. 🚀
